{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based wheel reward mechanic in which the player appears to stop on a valuable prize, but the genie then changes the result to another spin and the eventual reward is much lower. The wheel is therefore presented as a random reward-delivery system, supporting Reward Mania. The claim that the genie moves the wheel after it has stopped asserts that the game manipulates or overrides the apparent outcome, supporting Illusion of Control. No real-money stake is described in the review text, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag, and then the prices after the spin are way lower than the original price",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer claims the game changes the wheel outcome after it appears to stop, which describes concealed manipulation of how the result is determined."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "A wheel spin is a chance-based reward-delivery mechanic whose unpredictable outcomes sustain engagement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review attributes the inability to open gifts, trade, throw Poké Balls, and respond during battles to crashing, lag, freezing, and unresponsive controls. Under the technical-fault rule, these malfunction-attributed behaviors do not support dark-pattern labels. The review does explicitly describe PvP and Rocket battles involving an opponent, which establishes player competition, but it does not describe a design that forces continued play or penalizes exiting.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the pvp & rockets battles are also lagging switching out are too slow, charge attacks are not responding well and my game freeze while the opponent keep attacking",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes battles against an opponent, establishing competition between players or combatants despite the problems being attributed to lag and freezing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive matches against other players, including repeatedly facing specific cards and having to defend against them. It explicitly characterizes the game as pay to win and says that advancing higher is usually not possible through skill alone, implying that paid advantages affect progression or competitive success. The complaint that cards are buffed does not by itself establish Power Creep because the review does not say that previously purchased cards lost value after acquisition.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against other players using particular cards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win and usually doesn't let you get higher from just using skill",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that paid advantages are needed to advance to higher levels rather than relying only on skill."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a paid pass as an existing game feature, which is a battle pass and therefore a recurring-fee mechanic. It also describes players getting friends and joining a team to trade lives, which is a resource-exchange mechanic. The missing game element and the request for 9 diamonds are attributed to a game element not appearing and to customer-service compensation; the text does not clearly establish a deliberate reward or monetization pattern. The statement that players do not have to pay to win is not itself a mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "you don't buy from this game beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes, MMO-subscriptions",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly refers to an existing paid pass, which is a battle-pass mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Players exchange lives with one another, creating the resource-trading mechanic covered by Reciprocity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current game mechanics despite framing them as suggestions. Lives take a real-world time period to replenish, so the player must wait before continuing to play. The game also has daily prizes that reset when a day is missed, creating a daily-return incentive with a penalty for absence. The harder levels and general addictiveness are evaluations, not mechanics covered by the codebook.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The span implies that lives regenerate on an in-game timer, requiring the player to wait before playing again."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars; encourages return visits every day and punishes you for missing a day",
      "rationale": "Daily prizes are reset when the player misses a day, which is a daily reward mechanism with an explicit missed-day penalty."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally. The player is forced to remain on the ad screen, and tapping the screen redirects them to a store instead of closing the ad. The missing cross mark and misleading click behavior obstruct the opt-out and direct attention toward a purchase destination. Killing the game is the only way to leave, but the review attributes that consequence to the ad interface rather than a deliberate session-saving rule, so Mandatory Marathon does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to remain in an in-game advertisement because it cannot be dismissed."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The interface removes the normal close control and routes taps toward the store, obstructing the opt-out from the purchase-oriented screen."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
