  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes difficult levels taking a week to pass, which indicates slow progress but does not identify a repetitive task or an in-game timer, so it does not independently support Grinding or Playing by Appointment. It explicitly says that some tasks are impossible unless the player buys boosters, making payment the condition for progressing past those obstacles. No other game mechanic is clearly described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that progressing through certain tasks is impossible without purchasing boosters."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a timed event with a displayed two-day deadline and a reward of 525 dice. They were three steps from the reward, ran out of dice, and expected to return within the remaining event window, but after sleeping the event had changed. This is a game-clock schedule governing when the reward opportunity is available, so it supports Playing by Appointment. The review does not explicitly describe pressure to continue or anxiety about falling behind, so FOMO is not assigned. Running out of dice and mentioning F2P does not by itself establish payment-gated progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Fear of Missing Out: Where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event's stated timer set the window for obtaining the reward, but the opportunity changed when the player returned within that window."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a friend-invitation reward mechanic: the player is supposed to receive rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook, which uses social recruitment as an incentive. Because invitations are being sent to friends through Facebook, it also describes the game acting through the player's social graph. The sticker system gives repeated duplicate stickers, indicating randomized reward delivery with unwanted duplicate outcomes, so Reward Mania applies. The lack of luck while playing max bet does not clearly establish a paid wager or a gambling mechanic, and the inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it does not support a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme",
      "rationale": "The game incentivizes the player to recruit friends into the social/game ecosystem by offering rewards for their joining."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message",
      "rationale": "The span describes invitations being sent through the player's friend and Facebook social graph, which is messaging performed via the player's identity."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "The sticker reward system delivers repeated unwanted duplicates from a randomized collection, making the delivery mechanism sustain repeated attempts."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an interface rule in which opening the game or entering the 1v1 area automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-stakes table available, making an unintended tap likely to enter a costly match. That is UI Misdirection because the interface steers attention and selection toward the expensive option. The reviewer also says that losing the coins leaves players forced to buy more real ones, describing payment as necessary to restore continued access to play, which supports Pay to Progress. The 1v1 section is a player-versus-player competitive mode, so Competition is also explicitly present. The requested Default Table Lock and lowest-table default are hypothetical fixes and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins. This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically selects and presents the highest-cost table, steering the player toward an unwanted, expensive option and encouraging accidental mis-clicks."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The span describes losing the currency needed for play and then having to purchase more to continue."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "go to the 1v1 section",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "A 1v1 section is a game mode in which the player competes against another player."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a chance-based outcome system that the player believes is deliberately unfavorable: the RNG is said to be “against you,” which is an allegation that the game manipulates or predetermines outcomes rather than merely producing an unlucky result. The review also compares the cost of playing a level with the reward for winning, saying that at around level 100 the player spends 2200 and barely earns that amount back. This describes poor reward value and an unfavorable economy, but it does not explicitly state that payment is required to unlock progress, nor does it identify real money being converted into currency or a chance-based reward delivery system. Therefore, no monetary or reward-mechanic label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game’s random outcomes are systematically manipulated against the player, which describes concealed or rigged outcome determination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game as repetitive, but “Same old same old grinder” is a general evaluation without naming a repeated activity tied to progress, so it does not establish Grinding. The reviewer says ordinary players cannot win an event and explicitly characterizes the game as “pay to win,” with a reported $30,000 expenditure to win the last event. This describes payment granting an in-game advantage or competitive success, which supports Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. The review does not clearly describe players competing against one another as a mechanic, only winning an event, so Competition is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly states that spending money is the route to winning, describing payment as providing an in-game advantage."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. The game is explicitly pay-to-win, meaning spending money provides an in-game advantage. Base upgrades impose increasingly long update timers, which makes progress wait on the game's schedule. The statement that everything scales endlessly and there is no point to look forward to describes a game whose progression continually recedes rather than reaching a completion state, so it supports Infinite Treadmill; however, “more grind” does not name a repetitive activity specific enough for Grinding. The UI is described as deliberately confusing and overwhelming to push players toward purchases, which is UI Misdirection. Alliances and attacks create player-versus-player competition, but the review does not state that bullying or attacking produces a specific reward or advantage beyond ordinary competition, and being pressured to join an alliance for protection is not Forced Fellowship under the coerced-grouping boundary.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that spending money gives players an advantage in the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Base progress is gated by in-game update timers that require the player to wait."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review describes progression as endlessly expanding without an attainable endpoint."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The interface is intentionally designed to confuse and overwhelm players in order to direct them toward purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game places players against one another through attacks by other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that requires the player to place a 50,000-unit bet before the daily rewards can be completed. This is a daily return incentive with a requirement attached, so Daily Rewards applies. The bet is also a chance-based casino mechanic using an in-game stake; the review does not say the stake is purchased with real money, but the codebook permits Gambling when earned in-game currency is wagered without a purchase being described. The timed rewards and their 40,000 maximum describe reward amounts, not a wait timer or an unpredictable delivery system.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily log-in reward calendars / daily task lists with resetting rewards",
      "rationale": "The game provides daily rewards but requires a specified action before the daily reward cycle can be completed."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "unless I bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling; stake using earned or in-game currency without a purchase described",
      "rationale": "The player must wager an in-game stake in a casino-slots mechanic, making the bet a chance-based activity even though real-money purchase of the stake is not stated."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a sticker-collection game in which completing the collection is difficult because the last one or two stickers are effectively unavailable without spending a large amount of money. It also says that winning anything costs hundreds of dollars, tying payment to obtaining rewards or advancing toward completion. The statement that a goal may be completed only one out of a hundred times describes very low success from the reviewer’s perspective, but it does not explicitly identify a chance-based delivery mechanic, so it does not independently support Gambling or Reward Mania.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "Obtaining the remaining stickers and completing the collection is described as contingent on paying a large amount of money."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is pursuing completion of a sticker collection, with the missing final items preventing the set from being completed."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes increasingly difficult levels, running out of lives several times per day, and taking an average of 5–7 days to complete each level. The lives are a depleted resource, but no recharge timer or required waiting schedule is stated, so this does not establish Playing by Appointment. The review explicitly says that buying add-ons is necessary to get through the game and that not paying makes progress a waste of time, which describes payment as a condition for advancement. The reviewer mentions spending too much money and time but is uninstalling, so this does not show accumulated investment making it difficult to leave. No specific repetitive activity is described, so Grinding is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through a sane mind. it's pure time waste if you're not paying.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that progressing through the game requires buying add-ons and that the unpaid route is unreasonably burdensome."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. Progress is tied to payment: the player says free-to-play progress cannot keep up and that the newest bot must be bought with money rather than earned, which is Pay to Progress. Purchase offers appear every time the player logs in and block access to gameplay until dismissed, so they are purchase solicitations that intercept play and reduce the friction of buying, supporting Easy to Purchase. The review also says the only benefit of free-to-play rewards is watching ads, which implies rewarded advertisements. The shifting meta and newest bot do not by themselves establish Power Creep because the reviewer does not say that an item they already owned lost value, and no explicit competitive matchup is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "If you want to progress without paying money, goodluck! Even if you wanted to do f2p, you would not be able to keep up with shifting meta where you have to buy the newest bot with money instead of earning it.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative; Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The free route is described as unable to support progress or keep up, while payment is required to obtain the newest bot instead of earning it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "everytime you log in, your screen has spam of so called \"good deals\" in which I mean in app purchases before you can actually play your game.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Purchase solicitation",
      "rationale": "Purchase offers directly intercept gameplay on login and must be dealt with before the player can play."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the only time you benofit by getting f2p rewards is ads",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes advertisements as the required or incentivized route for obtaining free-to-play rewards."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a card-collection system in which card packs stop providing new cards when the player is close to completing a set, especially as the set approaches its expiration. The player is pursuing completion of the set and loses the completion reward because the needed cards no longer appear. The expiring collection also imposes a time window, and failure to complete it before expiration causes the reward to be forfeited. This supports Complete the Collection and Playing by Appointment. It does not establish an infinite treadmill, monetization, or a randomized reward-delivery mechanic strongly enough for Reward Mania.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "when one is almost close to completing a set, new cards don't appear again",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is trying to obtain the remaining cards needed to complete a collection set."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "especially when the set is almost expiring. So after collecting 125 cards from the beginning of the collection you will miss the reward for the set completion",
      "rule_applied": "vs Fear of Missing Out: where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment",
      "rationale": "The collection has a game-defined expiration deadline, and failing to complete it before that deadline causes the player to lose the completion reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a capped inventory system that obstructs play: the board and refrigerator have a fixed number of spaces, most are occupied by generator parts or unfinished/high-level items, and the player refuses to delete valuable items such as corn and chicken pie. This is realized wasted or stranded inventory value, so it supports Waste Aversion. The reviewer also says they cannot store and play without spending money to buy parts, implying that payment is presented as the route to continue progressing when the free inventory path is insufficient, which supports Pay to Progress. There is no explicit statement that accumulated progress is keeping the reviewer from quitting, so Easy to Get, Hard to Lose does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "Not enough slots to store and play without spending $$ to buy parts. I have 63 spots, on the board and 37 spots in the refrigerator. 98 spaces total, 72 are taken up by parts of generators or lower generators that I can't merge with higher generators yet or high level items like a corn that I am not going to delete. A lambchop can take up to 9 spaces or more of my 26 spaces and I'm not deleting the chicken pie",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Boundary rule vs NONE",
      "rationale": "The inventory cap is actively constraining play, with specific capacity consumed by items the reviewer will not delete, demonstrating stranded value and a concrete storage shortfall."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Not enough slots to store and play without spending $$ to buy parts",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The reviewer contrasts being unable to continue smoothly without spending money with buying parts as the paid route to keep playing and progressing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer is describing a collection goal: repeatedly playing bingo rounds to collect photos and complete a set. After losing a round, the game presents an offer to spend extra coins to obtain a missing photo, making payment an alternative route toward completing the collection. The reviewer says they paid but received no promised photo despite getting 2-3 bingos; this is a failure to deliver the offer, but they do not explicitly attribute it to a bug or technical fault. The review also says they wasted money, but it does not describe a specific leftover-currency shortfall or a chance-based reward mechanic clearly enough for Waste Aversion or Gambling.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "I play round after round to collect 'photos', and after each round that I lose it will ask me if I want to spend extra coins to claim a photo I don't have to complete the set.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is repeatedly pursuing missing photos in order to complete a set."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "ask me if I want to spend extra coins to claim a photo I don't have to complete the set",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / paid alternative",
      "rationale": "The game offers payment as a route to obtain a missing collection item needed for completion."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repetitive resource gathering as the activity required to level up, so it supports Grinding. It also describes alliances attacking smaller alliances and taking their resources; this establishes player-versus-player competition and portrays stronger groups benefiting from harmful, asymmetric attacks, supporting both Competition and Encourages Anti-Social Behavior. The reviewer mentions dedicating time to the game and alliances, but does not say this investment makes quitting difficult, so Easy to Get, Hard to Lose is not supported. The reference to an advertisement is too vague and appears to concern advertising rather than an in-game ad mechanic, so it does not support Advertisement.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "It's just grinding resources trying not to get raided so you can level up",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly describes repeatedly gathering resources as the activity needed for progress."
